          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "You should consult a healthcare provider regarding your HbA1c level of 7.2%. \n\nAn HbA1c of 7.2% indicates that your average blood sugar level has been higher than normal over the past two to three months, which suggests the presence of type 2 diabetes, as it is above the 6.5% threshold for diabetes diagnosis according to standard guidelines (source: condition-type-2-diabetes).\n\nThe general target HbA1c for individuals with diabetes is below 7%, although this can vary based on personal health considerations (source: guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). Some organizations advocate for individualized targets, advising that treatment goals should consider the person's overall health and risk factors (source: guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets).\n\nIn the ICD-10 classification, type 2 diabetes is recorded under the code **E11** (source: coding-type-2-diabetes-e11). This code covers various complications related to the disease as well.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
